               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate calendar="#gregorian" notAfter="1319" notBefore="1240">s. xiii
                        med.–xiv in.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#m"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_420">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Franciscan convent</orgName><p>The manuscript contains marginalia similar to
                        those in <ref target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_4220">BL
                           MS Harley 3249</ref>, and <ref
                           target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_596">Cambridge,
                           Corpus Christi College, MS 135</ref> (information from David Rundle). The
                        manuscript also contains the best witness to Robert Grosseteste's
                        translation of John of Damascus, leading to the surmise that it was probably
                        made by and belonged to the Oxford Franciscans, to whom Grosseteste left his
                        books. See further M. Holland, 'Robert Grosseteste's translation of John of
                        Damascus's The Dialogue of the Christian and the Saracen', in <title>Robert
                           Grosseteste and His Intellectual Milieu</title>, ed. J. Flood and others
                        (Toronto 2013), 248–93, at p. 253.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_434">Oxford, Oxfordshire. St
                        Mary Magdalen College</orgName><p>Given to Magdalen College at the end of s.
                        xv: 'Codex collegii beate marie magdalenes in oxonia Ex dono Magistri
                        Ioannis Sydnere quondam socii eiusdem collegii' (fol. 9v). John Sydner was a
                        fellow of the college c. 1475 and the donor of three other books in the
                        library (BRUO 1839).</p></provenance>
               </history>
